  "title": "Nucleophilic addition: cyanide and ethanal",
  "caption": "The cyanide carbon's lone pair attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon and the C=O π electrons move onto oxygen, giving an alkoxide. The alkoxide takes a proton from HCN, which regenerates cyanide. The flat carbonyl can be attacked from either face, so an unsymmetrical carbonyl gives a racemic product. Ethanal is unsymmetrical, so 2-hydroxypropanenitrile forms as a racemic mixture.",
  "alt": "Nucleophilic addition mechanism: cyanide attacks the carbonyl carbon of ethanal to give an alkoxide, which takes a proton from HCN to form 2-hydroxypropanenitrile.",
  "description": [
    "Stage 1: ethanal, CH₃ bonded to a carbon that is double-bonded to oxygen and carries a hydrogen, with a cyanide ion below it. A curly arrow goes from the lone pair on cyanide's carbon to the carbonyl carbon, and another from the C=O double bond onto the oxygen.",
    "Stage 2: the alkoxide intermediate, oxygen now single-bonded and negatively charged, with the nitrile group on the carbon, next to a molecule of HCN. A curly arrow goes from a lone pair on the O⁻ to the hydrogen of HCN, and another from the H–C bond of HCN onto its carbon.",
    "Stage 3: 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, CH₃CH(OH)CN, and a regenerated cyanide ion."
  ],
